(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-pigmented-skin-lesions-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pigmented Skin Lesions Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is solar lentigo associated with?","choices":["Neurofibromatosis type I and the risk of neurocutaneous melanosis in infancy","Older age, sun damage, ephelides, tanning and birth control use, and with actinic keratosis and skin cancers","Autosomal dominant inheritance in fair-skinned people with blonde or red hair","Autoimmune destruction of the melanocytes, with other autoimmune conditions found alongside it"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — 90% of people have them by age 50. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What information should be gathered before deciding vitiligo management?","choices":["A thorough examination with medical, social and family history and demographics","A skin biopsy of the leading edge sent for histology and antigen mapping","A dermoscopic photograph of every lesion recorded for later comparison","A minimal erythema dose test to ultraviolet A and ultraviolet B light"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — management is multifactorial and depends on the extent of disease. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is a blue naevus composed of, and who is most affected?","choices":["Hypertrophic melanocytes with increased basal melanin, affecting fair-skinned children of both sexes equally","Deeply pigmented spindle or epithelioid melanocytes in the dermis, affecting women more than men, most commonly in the twenties","Junctional melanocytes at the dermoepidermal junction, affecting men more than women, most commonly in the fifties","Neural-crest derived precursors migrating along neurovascular bundles, present at birth or shortly afterwards"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the group includes common, cellular, combined and atypical cellular blue lesions. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Describe the typical lesions of vitiligo and where they begin.","choices":["Asymptomatic white non-scaly macules and patches with distinct margins that fluoresce under a Wood's lamp, often starting on face, acral and genital areas","Asymptomatic light brown symmetric macules 3 to 5 mm that darken with ultraviolet exposure and fade in winter","Pruritic scaly hypopigmented patches on the trunk and the shoulders that show branching hyphae on a potassium hydroxide preparation","Well-defined depigmented patches following the lines of a dermatome and never crossing the midline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — distribution is usually symmetrical except in the segmental variant. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does histopathology show in ephelides?","choices":["An increased density of melanocytes with melanin macroglobules scattered through the epidermis","Deeply pigmented spindle or epithelioid melanocytes sitting within the dermis","Complete absence of melanocytes from the affected area of the epidermis","A normal to reduced number of hypertrophic melanocytes with increased melanin in the basal epidermal layer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — biopsy is not needed, as the diagnosis is clinical. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which factors are associated with photochemotherapy-induced lentigines?","choices":["A history of tanning bed use, obesity and hormonal contraceptive exposure","The total number of treatments, male sex, fair skin and older age","The dose of ultraviolet B given, female sex, dark skin and younger age","A family history of dysplastic naevi and the total number of naevi present"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — they appear on sun-protected sites such as buttocks and genitalia as well as exposed ones. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which investigations are used in vitiligo?","choices":["Skin scraping with mineral oil examined microscopically for the organism and its ova","Punch biopsy of every lesion sent for direct immunofluorescence and antigen mapping","Photopatch testing with two duplicate sets of allergens, one of which is then irradiated with ultraviolet A","Wood's lamp examination in a dark room, with a complete blood count and antinuclear antibody for associated autoimmune disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis itself is clinical. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Describe the typical appearance of a common acquired melanocytic naevus, and which feature is suspicious.","choices":["Usually under 1 cm, deeply blue-black and confined to the dorsal hands, scalp or sacral region; any pink colour is suspicious","Usually several millimetres to centimetres, pink or red, hairless and dome-shaped; rapid growth is suspicious","Usually under 6 mm with homogenous surface and colour, round to oval and sharply demarcated; very dark brown or black on light skin is suspicious","Usually over 10 mm with heterogeneous surface and colour, irregular and poorly demarcated; a uniform tan colour is suspicious"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they can occur anywhere on the body. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What does the background pigmentation of a naevus spilus resemble?","choices":["A blue naevus, deeply pigmented and blue-grey to blue-black","A café-au-lait spot, circumscribed with even light pigmentation","A solar lentigo, with irregular borders that coalesce at sunburn sites","A vitiligo patch, white and non-scaly with distinct margins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — scattered darker macules or papules sit superimposed on it. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"How are melanocytic naevi divided by origin?","choices":["Acquired naevi arise from junctional melanocytes; congenital naevi arise from neural-crest derived precursors migrating along neurovascular bundles","Acquired naevi arise from neural-crest derived precursors; congenital naevi arise from junctional melanocytes within the epidermis","Acquired naevi arise from keratinocytes of the basal layer; congenital naevi arise from hair follicle precursor cells in the dermis","Acquired naevi arise from dermal fibroblasts; congenital naevi arise from vascular endothelium along the neurovascular bundles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — dysplastic naevi are the group displaying atypical architectural and cytologic features. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"How are lentigines diagnosed and managed?","choices":["By Wood's lamp examination in a dark room, with phototherapy for extensive involvement","Clinically, with no treatment necessary; cosmetic removal by cryotherapy or quality-switched laser if the patient prefers","By biopsy in every single case, with excision required because of the risk of later malignant transformation","Clinically, with sun protection and topical depigmenting agents, avoiding cryotherapy entirely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lesions are benign. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Describe naevus spilus and its melanoma risk.","choices":["A collection of atypical naevi over 5 mm with irregular indistinct borders, which frequently progresses to melanoma","A spotted naevus with a tan café-au-lait-like background carrying scattered darker macules or papules, which rarely progresses to melanoma","A spotted naevus with a uniformly black background carrying scattered pale macules, which frequently progresses to melanoma","A group of deeply pigmented dermal lesions of spindle or epithelioid melanocytes, which rarely progresses to melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the background patch ranges from under 1 cm to over 10 cm, most often on trunk and extremities. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Why does the lecture caution against dismissing vitiligo as cosmetic?","choices":["It indicates an underlying internal malignancy in a substantial minority of patients","It always progresses to universal depigmentation if it is left untreated","It affects patients psychologically and socially, through low self-esteem and poor body image","It carries a high risk of transformation into melanoma within the depigmented areas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — psychological intervention is named as part of management. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How is pigmented spindle cell naevus diagnosed and managed?","choices":["Confirmed by biopsy, with excision and negative margins as management","Diagnosed clinically, with observation and periodic evaluation as management","Diagnosed clinically, with cryotherapy for cosmetic removal if preferred","Confirmed by biopsy, with topical depigmenting agents as management"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lesion is benign despite its jet-black appearance. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How do ephelides behave over time and with sun exposure?","choices":["They first appear in young children, darken with ultraviolet exposure, fade when exposure stops, and regress later in life","They first appear after the age of fifty, darken steadily thereafter, and do not fade when sun exposure stops","They appear at birth or shortly after and enlarge in proportion to the child's growth over time","They appear in adolescence, peak in number during the thirties, and then decline steadily"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they are more pronounced in spring and summer and fade in winter. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why does Spitz naevus require particular care, and what does the presence of multiple lesions suggest?","choices":["It resembles a blue naevus, and multiple lesions suggest neurocutaneous melanosis is present","It sometimes resembles melanoma, and multiple lesions can be associated with a familial cancer syndrome","It always transforms into melanoma, and multiple lesions indicate that transformation has occurred","It resembles a seborrheic keratosis, and multiple lesions suggest an internal malignancy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — diagnosis is by biopsy or wide excision, and management is by excision. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Describe dermatosis papulosa nigrans and its epidemiology.","choices":["Multiple white non-scaly macules with distinct margins, beginning before the age of thirty, with males and females equally affected","Multiple darkly pigmented macules within a tan background patch on the trunk, present at birth or in the first years of life","Multiple smooth firm black or dark brown papules 1 to 5 mm on face and neck, commonest in African Americans, dark-skinned Asians and Polynesians, with females affected more than males","Multiple light brown macules 3 to 5 mm on sun-exposed skin, commonest in fair-skinned people with possible Celtic ancestry, with males and females equally affected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lesions are identical to small seborrheic keratoses. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which non-pharmacologic elements are named in the management of vitiligo?","choices":["Sun protection counselling with a broad-spectrum sunscreen used daily","Weight loss, smoking cessation and avoidance of constrictive clothing","Household washing at high temperature and treatment of all contacts","Psychological intervention, cosmetic therapies and non-traditional approaches"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the psychological and social impact is emphasised. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why do seborrheic keratoses matter clinically in older adults, given that they are benign?","choices":["They are easily mistaken for neoplasms","They frequently transform into melanoma","They indicate an underlying malignancy","They spread from person to person"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is clinical and management is supportive. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Describe the clinical appearance of lentigines and where they occur.","choices":["Small light brown symmetric macules 3 to 5 mm across, appearing on sun-exposed skin in fair individuals","Beige to brown to black papules that feel velvety and look stuck onto the surface of the skin","Well-circumscribed round to oval uniformly black or brown macules under 5 mm, on skin, conjunctiva and mucocutaneous surfaces","Well-defined macules with irregular borders that coalesce at sites of severe sunburn, from under 1 mm to several centimetres"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they occur on both sun-exposed and sun-protected areas. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Into what can solar lentigines progress over time?","choices":["Dysplastic naevi","Blue naevi","Lichenoid keratoses","Seborrheic keratoses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they may also enlarge, darken, stay stable or regress. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which imaging is indicated for a congenital melanocytic naevus on the cranium or axial midline?","choices":["Whole-body positron emission tomography to screen for metastatic melanoma at presentation","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain, with or without total spine, matched to the anatomic location of the naevus","Computed tomography of the affected area, to define the depth and the extent of the pigmented lesion","Ultrasound of the lesion to assess the thickness of the pigmented tissue before any excision"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the concern is neurocutaneous melanosis. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which single feature best separates ephelides from lentigines?","choices":["Ephelides occur only on sun-exposed skin, whereas lentigines do not","Ephelides require biopsy for diagnosis, whereas lentigines do not","Ephelides fade when sun exposure stops, whereas lentigines do not","Ephelides are darker than lentigines and have irregular borders"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — lentigines are the main differential diagnosis for ephelides. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"When should an inherited disorder be considered in a patient with lentigines?","choices":["When a single lesion is present on a sun-exposed site","When the lesions fade completely during the winter months","When the lesions are confined to the conjunctiva only","When a partial or generalised lentigo is present"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — LAMB and myxoma syndrome are the named examples of inherited associations. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Describe the appearance of a dysplastic melanocytic naevus.","choices":["At least 5 mm in diameter with irregular indistinct borders and variable tan to brown pigmentation, with a smooth or pebbly surface","Under 6 mm in diameter with sharp demarcation and homogenous colour, round to oval in shape","Under 1 cm in diameter, deeply pigmented blue to blue-black, on dorsal hands, scalp or sacrum","Beige to black papules and plaques 2 to 20 mm across that feel velvety and appear stuck onto the surface of the skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they are common on sun-exposed skin and may progress to melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How do common and cellular blue naevi differ?","choices":["Common blue naevi are larger plaques over 1 cm arising before age forty; cellular blue naevi are small lesions under 1 cm arising in adolescence","Common blue naevi occur on the palms and soles; cellular blue naevi occur on the trunk and the extremities only","Common blue naevi are present at birth; cellular blue naevi develop only after the fifth decade of life","Common blue naevi are deeply pigmented lesions under 1 cm arising in adolescence; cellular blue naevi are larger plaques or nodules over 1 cm arising before age forty"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — both occur on dorsal hands and feet, scalp, buttocks or sacral region. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What relationship does the lecture draw between naevus count and melanoma risk?","choices":["The higher the number of naevi, the lower the risk of melanoma","Naevus count has no bearing at all on the risk of melanoma","Only naevi over 10 mm contribute to the risk of melanoma","The higher the number of naevi, the higher the risk of melanoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — in dysplastic naevus syndrome there can be over one hundred by adolescence. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Describe the clinical appearance of congenital melanocytic naevi.","choices":["Sharply circumscribed jet-black papules under 7 mm across, sometimes with shades of blue, grey or brown","Solitary pink or red hairless dome-shaped firm papules that grow and then stabilise over time","Deeply pigmented blue to blue-black macules under 1 cm across on the dorsal hands, scalp or sacrum","Flat brown patches or plaques with smooth or slightly uneven borders, sometimes pebbly, rugose, verrucous or lobular"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — they are evident at birth or shortly after. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Describe the natural history of common acquired melanocytic naevi.","choices":["They develop slowly after birth, enlarge symmetrically, stabilise and regress, peaking in number in the thirties then declining","They are present at birth, enlarge in proportion to the child's own growth, and never regress at any stage of life","They appear only after the age of fifty, enlarge steadily, and do not fade when sun exposure ends","They appear in early childhood, darken with ultraviolet exposure and fade during the winter months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — melanoma risk rises with the number of lesions. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How are ephelides inherited, and in whom do they most commonly appear?","choices":["X-linked recessive, most commonly in males with a family history of melanoma or dysplastic nevi","Sporadic and non-inherited, most commonly in older adults with a long history of sun damage","Autosomal dominant, most commonly in fair-skinned people with blonde or red hair and possibly Celtic ancestry","Autosomal recessive, most commonly in dark-skinned people of African, Asian or Polynesian ancestry"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — males and females are equally affected. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 4"}]};
